**P2 — cross-body jump/branch-false validator rule.**

Added `jump-scope-rule` to `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc` (right before `register-bounds-rule`): for every `:jump`/`:branch-false` instruction, compares `(get owner pc)` (the instruction's own body, via the existing `owner-of` helper) against `(get owner target)` (the jump target's body); refuses with `{:rule :jump-scope, :pc pc}` if they differ. `:closure` targets are untouched (different operand, already checked by `body-scope-rule`). Wired into `body-rules` immediately after `body-scope-rule` (the first rule that makes `owner-of` meaningful) and before `register-bounds-rule`/`terminator-rule`/the four live-set rules, per the task's placement instruction, with the `body-rules` docstring and the namespace-level validator docstring both updated to name it.

Test: `validator-rejects-cross-body-jump-target` in `debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc`. Builds `(app (lam [x] (tail x)) (if-node true 1 2))`, which lowers to two bodies (body 0 pcs 0-7 with an in-body `:jump` at pc 4, body 1 pcs 8-9). Confirms the unmodified image passes (`nil? register-image-defect`), then rewrites the `:jump`'s target to body 1's `:start` (8) — a bounds-valid pc per `target-bounds-rule` but the wrong body — and confirms `register-image-defect` now returns `{:rule :jump-scope ...}`. Existing `validator-accepts-every-corpus-image` and `validator-accepts-b2-fixtures` (which include `if-program`, containing legitimate within-body `:branch-false`/`:jump`) still pass, confirming no false positive against legitimate within-body jumps.

